			<task label="hw-while-digit-count">
				<title>★ Counting Digits</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function <pf>numDigits(n)</pf> that takes a positive integer <pf>n</pf> and returns how many digits it has. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop that repeatedly divides <pf>n</pf> by 10 (using <pf>floor</pf>) until it reaches 0, counting each step.
					</p>
					<p>
						For example:
						<ul>
							<li><pf>numDigits(7)</pf> returns <pf>1</pf></li>
							<li><pf>numDigits(42)</pf> returns <pf>2</pf></li>
							<li><pf>numDigits(10000)</pf> returns <pf>5</pf></li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						function count = numDigits(n)
							count = 0;
							while n &gt; 0
								n = floor(n / 10);
								count = count + 1;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="hw-while-int-sqrt">
				<title>★★ Integer Square Root</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function <pf>intSqrt(n)</pf> that returns the largest integer whose square is less than or equal to <pf>n</pf>. This is the <term>integer square root</term>, sometimes written <m>\lfloor \sqrt{n} \rfloor</m>. Do <em>not</em> use <pf>sqrt</pf>, <pf>^</pf>, or any other built-in power/root function.
					</p>
					<p>
						Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop that starts with <pf>k = 0</pf> and increments <pf>k</pf> as long as <pf>(k+1)*(k+1) &lt;= n</pf>.
					</p>
					<p>
						For example:
						<ul>
							<li><pf>intSqrt(25)</pf> returns <pf>5</pf></li>
							<li><pf>intSqrt(26)</pf> returns <pf>5</pf></li>
							<li><pf>intSqrt(99)</pf> returns <pf>9</pf></li>
							<li><pf>intSqrt(100)</pf> returns <pf>10</pf></li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						function k = intSqrt(n)
							k = 0;
							while (k + 1) * (k + 1) &lt;= n
								k = k + 1;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="hw-while-base-conversion0">
				<title>★★ Decimal to Binary Conversion</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						To convert a positive integer from decimal to binary, repeatedly divide by 2 and record the remainders. The remainders, read in reverse order, form the binary representation. For example:
						<md>
							<mrow>13 \div 2 = 6 \text{ remainder } 1</mrow>
							<mrow>6 \div 2 = 3 \text{ remainder } 0</mrow>
							<mrow>3 \div 2 = 1 \text{ remainder } 1</mrow>
							<mrow>1 \div 2 = 0 \text{ remainder } 1</mrow>
						</md>
						Reading the remainders bottom-to-top gives <m>13_{10} = 1101_2</m>.
					</p>
					<p>
						Write a function <pf>toBinary(n)</pf> that takes a positive integer and returns its binary representation as a decimal number whose digits are the binary digits. For example, <pf>toBinary(13)</pf> returns <pf>1101</pf> (the number one-thousand-one-hundred-and-one, whose digits happen to be the binary representation of 13).
					</p>
					<p>
						<em>Hint:</em> To build the result in the correct order, use a variable <pf>place</pf> that starts at 1 and is multiplied by 10 each iteration. Each remainder is added as <pf>result = result + remainder * place</pf>.
					</p>
					<p>
						Verify: <pf>toBinary(1)</pf> returns <pf>1</pf>, <pf>toBinary(10)</pf> returns <pf>1010</pf>, and <pf>toBinary(255)</pf> returns <pf>11111111</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						function result = toBinary(n)
							result = 0;
							place = 1;
							while n &gt; 0
								remainder = mod(n, 2);
								result = result + remainder * place;
								n = floor(n / 2);
								place = place * 10;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="hw-while-digital-root">
				<title>Digital Root</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that computes the digital root of a positive integer <pf>n</pf>. The digital root 
						is found by repeatedly summing the digits until only a single digit remains.
					</p>
					<p>
						Use an outer <pf>while</pf> loop that continues as long as <pf>n &gt;= 10</pf>. Inside this loop:
						first set a variable (for example, <pf>sum_digits</pf>) to 0. Then copy <pf>n</pf> into a temporary 
						variable (for example, <pf>temp</pf>) and use an inner <pf>while</pf> loop that runs while <pf>temp &gt; 0</pf>.
						In the inner loop, extract the rightmost digit with <pf>mod(temp, 10)</pf>, add it to <pf>sum_digits</pf>, 
						and then update <pf>temp</pf> using <pf>floor(temp/10)</pf> to remove the rightmost digit. After the inner 
						loop finishes, assign <pf>n = sum_digits</pf> and repeat the outer loop test. When the outer loop ends, 
						store the final single-digit value of <pf>n</pf> in <pf>digital_root</pf>.
					</p>

					<tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
						<col width="12%"/>
						<col width="88%" halign="left"/>
						<row>
							<cell>Inputs:</cell>
							<cell>
								<p><pf>n</pf> (1x1) double — positive integer</p>
							</cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell>Outputs:</cell>
							<cell>
								<p><pf>digital_root</pf> (1x1) double — single digit (0-9)</p>
							</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>

					<p>Test cases:</p>

					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1: Multi-digit number
						n = 38;
						% Run your script
						% Expected: digital_root = 2
						% (38 → 3+8=11 → 1+1=2)

						% Test 2: Another example
						n = 987;
						% Run your script
						% Expected: digital_root = 6
						% (987 → 9+8+7=24 → 2+4=6)

						% Test 3: Single digit
						n = 7;
						% Run your script
						% Expected: digital_root = 7

						% Test 4: Larger number
						n = 12345;
						% Run your script
						% Expected: digital_root = 6
						% (12345 → 1+2+3+4+5=15 → 1+5=6)
					</program>

				</statement>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-while-digital-root-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						<term>Digit Extraction.</term> The rightmost digit of an integer (i.e., the singles digit) can be extracted by careful use of the <pf>mod</pf> function. Write a simple function named <pf>lastDigit</pf> that takes a positive integer <pf>n</pf> and returns its rightmost digit. For example, <pf>lastDigit(123)</pf> should return <pf>3</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function digit = lastDigit(n)
							digit = mod(n, 10);
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-while-digital-root-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						<term>Digit Removal.</term> The rightmost digit of an integer (i.e., the singles digit) can be removed by moving the decimal point to the left and rounding down. Write a simple function named <pf>removeLastDigit</pf> that takes a positive integer <pf>n</pf> and returns the result of removing its rightmost digit. For example, <pf>removeLastDigit(123)</pf> should return <pf>12</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function digit = removeLastDigit(n)
							digit = floor(n/10);
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-while-digital-root-c">
				<statement>
					<p>
						<term>Digital Root.</term> The <term>digital root</term> of a number is found by summing its digits. For example, the digital root of 9875 is found by:
						<md>
							<mrow>9 + 8 + 7 + 5 = 29</mrow>
							<mrow>2 + 9 = 11</mrow>
							<mrow>1 + 1 = 2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>digitalRoot</pf>	that returns the digital root of any positive integer <pf>n</pf> using a <pf>while</pf>-loop. Use <pf>lastDigit</pf> and <pf>removeLastDigit</pf> as helper functions.
					</p>

					<p>
						Test your script with numbers like 38, 99, 123456, and 999999. What patterns do you notice?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function root = digitalRoot(n)
							root = n;
							while root &gt; 1
								last = lastDigit(root);
								root = removeLastDigit(root) + last;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="coding-exercises-while-loop-games">
			<title>Games With While Loops</title>

			<task label="activity-loop-if-points-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that runs a <pf>while</pf>-loop for 12 rounds. Use a loop counter variable (for example <c>round = 1</c>) initialized before the loop, a condition like <c>while round &lt;= 12</c>, and increment the counter each iteration. Each round generates a random scalar <pf>roll = randi([1, 12])</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						round = 1;
						while round &lt;= 12
							roll = randi([1, 12]);
							fprintf('Round %i: roll = %i\n', round, roll);
							round = round + 1;
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-loop-if-points-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Before the loop starts, initialize <pf>score = 0</pf>. Inside the loop, use an <pf>if-else</pf> statement: if <pf>mod(roll, 3) == 0</pf>, add 4 points to <pf>score</pf>; otherwise subtract 1 point.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						round = 1;
						score = 0;
						while round &lt;= 12
							roll = randi([1, 12]);
							fprintf('Round %i: roll = %i\n', round, roll);
							if mod(roll, 3) == 0
							score = score + 4;
							else
							score = score - 1;
							end
							round = round + 1;
						end
						fprintf('Final score: %i\n', score);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-loop-if-points-c">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Before the loop starts, initialize two scalar counters named <pf>bonusCount</pf> and <pf>penaltyCount</pf> to 0, then use them to track how many times each branch executes and finally display all three final values.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						round = 1;
						score = 0;
						bonusCount = 0;
						penaltyCount = 0;
						while round &lt;= 12
							roll = randi([1, 12]);
							fprintf('Round %i: roll = %i\n', round, roll);
							if mod(roll, 3) == 0
							score = score + 4;
							bonusCount = bonusCount + 1;
							else
							score = score - 1;
							penaltyCount = penaltyCount + 1;
							end
							round = round + 1;
						end
						fprintf('Final score: %i\n', score);
						fprintf('Bonus rolls: %i\n', bonusCount);
						fprintf('Penalty rolls: %i\n', penaltyCount);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="hw-while-fizzbuzz-count">
				<title>FizzBuzz Counting</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function <pf>fizzBuzzCount(N)</pf> that returns three outputs: the number of Fizzes, Buzzes, and FizzBuzzes in the range 1 to <pf>N</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						function [fizzCount, buzzCount, fbCount] = fizzBuzzCount(N)
							fizzCount = 0;
							buzzCount = 0;
							fbCount = 0;
							k = 1;
							while k &lt;= N
								if mod(k, 15) == 0
									fbCount = fbCount + 1;
								elseif mod(k, 3) == 0
									fizzCount = fizzCount + 1;
								elseif mod(k, 5) == 0
									buzzCount = buzzCount + 1;
								end
								k = k + 1;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="hw-while-base-conversion">
			<title>★★ Exploring Digits</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					The number <m>173</m> has three digits, each with its own place: 
					<ul>
						<li><m>1</m> - the <q>Hundreds Place</q></li>
						<li><m>7</m> - the <q>Tens Place</q></li>
						<li><m>3</m> - the <q>Ones Place</q></li>
					</ul>					
					The <term>place</term> tell us what multiplier to use for each digit:
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center">
					<row><cell><term>Place</term></cell><cell><term>Digit</term></cell><cell><term>Multiplier</term></cell><cell/><cell><term>Contribution</term></cell></row>
					<row><cell>Hundreds</cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>100</m></cell><cell><m>(10^2)</m></cell><cell><m>1 \times 100 = 100</m></cell></row>
					<row><cell>Tens</cell><cell><m>7</m></cell><cell><m>10</m></cell><cell><m>(10^1)</m></cell><cell><m>7 \times 10 = 70</m></cell></row>
					<row><cell>Ones</cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>(10^0)</m></cell><cell><m>3 \times 1 = 3</m></cell></row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					You construct the original number by adding up each contribution:
					<me>173 = 1 \cdot \underbracket{100}_{10^2} + 7 \cdot \underbracket{10}_{10^1} + 3 \cdot \underbracket{1}_{10^0}</me>.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="hw-base-conversion-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that constructs <m>x</m>, an <m>n</m>-digit number from <m>n</m> randomly generated digits. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to generate each digit with <pf>randi([0, 9])</pf>. Assume that the first digit is the ones place, the second digit is the tens place, and so on. 
					</p>
					<p>
						For example, suppose <m>n=4</m> and the random digits are <m>7</m>, <m>3</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>5</m>, then you are simply computing the sum:
						<me>x = 7 \cdot 10^0 + 3 \cdot 10^1 + 0 \cdot 10^2 + 5 \cdot 10^3 = 5037</me>.
						Print the variables as they are computed at each step, where:
						<ul>
							<li><pf>x</pf> is the current value of the number being constructed</li>
							<li><pf>x0</pf> is the previous value of <pf>x</pf> (for printing)</li>
							<li><pf>k</pf> is the loop index (use for place value)</li>
							<li><pf>digit</pf> is the random digit generated at each <pf>k</pf></li>
						</ul>
						Complete the pseudocode below to implement this idea.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% Initialize x, x0, k, and n
							while false		% condition to repeat
								% generate a random digit
								% print "k = ?,  digit = ?:  "
								% compute x
								% print "x = ? + ?•10^? = ?\n"
								% update x0 and k for the next iteration
							end
							fprintf('Final number: %i\n', x)
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = 0; x0 = 0; k = 0; n = 4;
						while k &lt; n		% condition to repeat
							digit = randi([0, 9]);
							fprintf('k = %i,  digit = %i:  ', k, digit);
							x = x + digit * 10^k;
							fprintf('x = %4i + %i•10^%i = %i\n', x0, digit, k, x);
							x0 = x; k = k + 1;
						end
						fprintf('Final number: %i\n', x)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="hw-base-conversion-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						The ones digit of <m>173</m> can be extracted with a two step process: 
						<ul>
							<li>Get the ones digit (<m>3</m>) as the remainder after dividing <m>173</m> by <m>10</m>.</li>
							<li>Remove the ones digit of <m>173</m> by dividing by <m>10</m> &amp; rounding down: <m>\frac{173}{10} = 17.3 \to 17</m>.</li>
						</ul>					
						Repeating this on <m>17</m> extracts <m>7</m>, leaving <m>1</m>. 
						Repeating again extracts <m>1</m>, leaving <m>0</m>. 
						The process stops when you are left with <m>0</m>.
					</p>
					<p>
						Write a function called <pf>printDigits</pf> that prints the digits of a given number <m>x</m>. 
						Code this using a <pf>while</pf>-loop together with the strategy above.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							function printDigits(n)
								% ==== Type your code here ====
								

							end
							% Test your function with the following calls:
							fprintf('\n Test 1: ')
							printDigits(1234)
							fprintf('\n Test 2: ')
							printDigits(50374254)
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function printDigits(n)
							while n &gt; 0
								digit = mod(n, 10);
								fprintf('%i', digit);
								n = floor(n / 10);
							end
						end
						% Test your function with the following calls:
						fprintf('\n Test 1: ')
						printDigits(1234)
						fprintf('\n Test 2: ')
						printDigits(50374254)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="hw-base-conversion-c">
				<title>Putting it together</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Now combine both ideas. Write a function <pf>toBinary(n)</pf> that takes a positive integer and returns its binary representation as a decimal number whose digits are the binary digits. For example, <pf>toBinary(13)</pf> returns <pf>1101</pf>.
					</p>
					<p>
						The structure is almost identical to <pf>reverseDigits</pf>, but instead of dividing by 10 and extracting base-10 digits, you divide by 2 and extract base-2 digits. The <pf>place</pf> variable still multiplies by 10 each step (because you are building a base-10 number whose digits happen to represent binary).
					</p>
					<p>
						Verify: <pf>toBinary(1)</pf> returns <pf>1</pf>, <pf>toBinary(10)</pf> returns <pf>1010</pf>, and <pf>toBinary(255)</pf> returns <pf>11111111</pf>.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							function result = toBinary(n)
								result = 0;
								place = 1;
								% ==== Type your code here ====
								

								% =============================
							end

							fprintf('%i\n', toBinary(1))
							fprintf('%i\n', toBinary(13))
							fprintf('%i\n', toBinary(10))
							fprintf('%i\n', toBinary(255))
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function result = toBinary(n)
							result = 0;
							place = 1;
							while n &gt; 0
								remainder = mod(n, 2);
								result = result + remainder * place;
								n = floor(n / 2);
								place = place * 10;
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-euclidean-algorithm">
			<title>The Euclidean Algorithm</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					The <term>Euclidean algorithm</term> computes the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two positive integers. It is one of the oldest known algorithms, appearing in Euclid's <em>Elements</em> around 300 BCE. The key idea is that <m>\gcd(a, b) = \gcd(b, \text{mod}(a, b))</m>. By repeatedly replacing the larger number with the remainder, the pair shrinks until the remainder is zero, at which point the other number is the GCD.
				</p>
				<p>
					For example, <m>\gcd(48, 18)</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>\gcd(48, 18): \quad 48 = 2 \cdot 18 + 12 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \gcd(18, 12)</mrow>
						<mrow>\gcd(18, 12): \quad 18 = 1 \cdot 12 + 6 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \gcd(12, 6)</mrow>
						<mrow>\gcd(12, 6): \quad 12 = 2 \cdot 6 + 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \gcd(6, 0)</mrow>
					</md>
					When the remainder hits 0, we stop. The GCD is 6.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-euclidean-a">
				<title>Implement the algorithm</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that computes <m>\gcd(a, b)</m> using a <pf>while</pf>-loop. The loop should continue as long as <pf>b ~= 0</pf>. Each iteration replaces <pf>a</pf> with <pf>b</pf> and <pf>b</pf> with <pf>mod(a, b)</pf>. Be careful about the order of these assignments.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							a = 48; b = 18;
							fprintf('gcd(%i, %i):\n', a, b)
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							

							% =============================
							fprintf('GCD = %i\n', a)
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution. Note that we must store <pf>mod(a, b)</pf> in a temporary variable before overwriting <pf>a</pf>.
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						a = 48; b = 18;
						fprintf('gcd(%i, %i):\n', a, b)
						% ==== Type your code here ====
						while b ~= 0
							r = mod(a, b);
							fprintf('  gcd(%i, %i) -> remainder %i\n', a, b, r)
							a = b;
							b = r;
						end
						% =============================
						fprintf('GCD = %i\n', a)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-euclidean-b">
				<title>Step counts and Fibonacci</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add a counter to track how many iterations the algorithm takes. Test your code with the following pairs:
						<ul>
							<li><m>\gcd(100, 36)</m></li>
							<li><m>\gcd(1000000, 7)</m></li>
							<li><m>\gcd(89, 55)</m> (consecutive Fibonacci numbers)</li>
							<li><m>\gcd(233, 144)</m> (consecutive Fibonacci numbers)</li>
						</ul>
						Which pairs take the most steps? What pattern do you notice?
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-euclidean-c">
				<title>Turn it into a function</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a function named <pf>myGCD</pf> that takes two positive integers <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf> and returns their GCD. Verify that <pf>myGCD(270, 192)</pf> returns 6 and that <pf>myGCD(17, 13)</pf> returns 1 (since 17 and 13 are coprime).
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						function result = myGCD(a, b)
							while b ~= 0
								r = mod(a, b);
								a = b;
								b = r;
							end
							result = a;
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-random-walk">
			<title>Random Walk and Gambler's Ruin</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					A <term>random walk</term> is a mathematical model in which a value changes by random steps at each point in time. Random walks appear throughout probability, physics, biology, and finance. In the simplest version, a walker starts at position 0 and at each step moves either +1 or −1 with equal probability.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-random-walk-a">
				<title>Walk until you escape</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that simulates a random walk starting at <pf>pos = 0</pf>. Each step, generate a random move using <pf>randi([0, 1]) * 2 - 1</pf> (this produces <m>-1</m> or <m>+1</m> with equal probability). Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop that continues until <pf>abs(pos) >= 10</pf> (i.e., the walker reaches +10 or −10). Count the number of steps taken.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							pos = 0;
							steps = 0;
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							

							% =============================
							fprintf('Reached %i in %i steps\n', pos, steps)
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						pos = 0;
						steps = 0;
						while abs(pos) &lt; 10
							move = randi([0, 1]) * 2 - 1;
							pos = pos + move;
							steps = steps + 1;
						end
						fprintf('Reached %i in %i steps\n', pos, steps)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-random-walk-b">
				<title>Gambler's ruin</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						The <term>Gambler's Ruin</term> problem is a classic result in probability. A gambler starts with <m>\$5</m> and plays a fair coin-flip game: heads wins <m>\$1</m>, tails loses <m>\$1</m>. The gambler plays until they are either broke (<m>\$0</m>) or reach their goal (<m>\$10</m>).
					</p>
					<p>
						Modify your random walk code so that <pf>pos</pf> starts at 5 and the loop runs until <pf>pos == 0</pf> or <pf>pos == 10</pf>. Track the number of steps and whether the gambler won or went broke.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							pos = 5;
							steps = 0;
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							

							% =============================
							if pos == 10
								fprintf('Won in %i steps!\n', steps)
							else
								fprintf('Went broke in %i steps.\n', steps)
							end
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						pos = 5;
						steps = 0;
						while pos &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; pos &lt; 10
							move = randi([0, 1]) * 2 - 1;
							pos = pos + move;
							steps = steps + 1;
						end
						if pos == 10
							fprintf('Won in %i steps!\n', steps)
						else
							fprintf('Went broke in %i steps.\n', steps)
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-random-walk-c">
				<title>Tracking extremes</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add variables <pf>maxPos</pf> and <pf>minPos</pf> to track the highest and lowest positions reached during the walk. Run your simulation a few times. How close does the gambler come to winning before going broke (or vice versa)?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						pos = 5;
						steps = 0;
						maxPos = pos;
						minPos = pos;
						while pos &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; pos &lt; 10
							move = randi([0, 1]) * 2 - 1;
							pos = pos + move;
							steps = steps + 1;
							if pos &gt; maxPos
								maxPos = pos;
							end
							if pos &lt; minPos
								minPos = pos;
							end
						end
						if pos == 10
							fprintf('Won in %i steps!\n', steps)
						else
							fprintf('Went broke in %i steps.\n', steps)
						end
						fprintf('Highest: $%i, Lowest: $%i\n', maxPos, minPos)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-while-guessing-game">
			<title>Number Guessing Game</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					In this activity, you will build a number guessing game where the computer picks a secret number and the player tries to guess it. The game uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to keep asking for guesses until the player finds the answer. <em>Note:</em> These activities use the <pf>input</pf> command and must be run in MATLAB (not in the browser).
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-guessing-game-a">
				<title>Basic game</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that generates a random integer between 1 and 100 using <pf>randi([1, 100])</pf> and stores it in <pf>secret</pf>. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to repeatedly prompt the player for a guess using <pf>input('Your guess: ')</pf>. After each guess, print whether the secret number is higher or lower. The loop should exit when the guess equals the secret number.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						secret = randi([1, 100]);
						guess = -1;
						attempts = 0;

						fprintf('I picked a number between 1 and 100.\n')

						while guess ~= secret
							guess = input('Your guess: ');
							attempts = attempts + 1;
							if guess &lt; secret
								fprintf('Too low!\n')
							elseif guess &gt; secret
								fprintf('Too high!\n')
							else
								fprintf('Correct! You got it in %i guesses.\n', attempts)
							end
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-guessing-game-c">
				<title>Limited guesses</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Modify your game so the player only gets 7 guesses. Update the <pf>while</pf>-loop condition to also check the number of attempts. If the player runs out of guesses, reveal the secret number.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						secret = randi([1, 100]);
						guess = -1;
						attempts = 0;
						maxAttempts = 7;

						fprintf('I picked a number between 1 and 100. You have %i guesses.\n', maxAttempts)

						while guess ~= secret &amp;&amp; attempts &lt; maxAttempts
							fprintf('Guesses remaining: %i\n', maxAttempts - attempts)
							guess = input('Your guess: ');
							attempts = attempts + 1;
							if guess &lt; secret
								fprintf('Too low!\n')
							elseif guess &gt; secret
								fprintf('Too high!\n')
							else
								fprintf('Correct! You got it in %i guesses.\n', attempts)
							end
						end

						if guess ~= secret
							fprintf('Out of guesses! The number was %i.\n', secret)
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-while-mental-math">
			<title>Mental Math Trainer</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Build a mental math quiz that generates random problems for the player to solve. The game continues as long as the player keeps answering correctly — one wrong answer ends the streak.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-mental-math-a">
				<title>Multiplication streak</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to quiz the player on multiplication. Each round, generate two random integers between 2 and 12 using <pf>randi</pf>, display the problem, and ask for the player's answer using <pf>input</pf>. If correct, increase the score and continue. If wrong, display the correct answer and end the game. Track and display the player's streak.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						streak = 0;
						correct = true;

						fprintf('=== Multiplication Streak ===\n')
						fprintf('Keep going until you miss one!\n\n')

						while correct
							a = randi([2, 12]);
							b = randi([2, 12]);
							fprintf('  %i x %i = ?\n', a, b)
							answer = input('  Your answer: ');

							if answer == a * b
								streak = streak + 1;
								fprintf('  Right! Streak: %i\n\n', streak)
							else
								correct = false;
								fprintf('  Wrong! %i x %i = %i\n', a, b, a * b)
							end
						end

						fprintf('\nFinal streak: %i\n', streak)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-mental-math-b">
				<title>Mixed operations</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Upgrade your trainer to randomly choose between addition, subtraction, and multiplication each round. Use <pf>randi([1, 3])</pf> to pick the operation and a <pf>switch</pf>-statement to generate the appropriate problem and compute the correct answer. For subtraction, make sure the first number is larger so the answer is non-negative.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						streak = 0;
						correct = true;

						fprintf('=== Mixed Math Streak ===\n\n')

						while correct
							op = randi([1, 3]);
							switch op
								case 1
									a = randi([10, 99]);
									b = randi([10, 99]);
									symbol = '+';
									trueAnswer = a + b;
								case 2
									a = randi([20, 99]);
									b = randi([1, a]);
									symbol = '-';
									trueAnswer = a - b;
								case 3
									a = randi([2, 12]);
									b = randi([2, 12]);
									symbol = '*';
									trueAnswer = a * b;
							end

							fprintf('  %i %s %i = ?\n', a, symbol, b)
							answer = input('  Your answer: ');

							if answer == trueAnswer
								streak = streak + 1;
								fprintf('  Right! Streak: %i\n\n', streak)
							else
								correct = false;
								fprintf('  Wrong! The answer was %i.\n', trueAnswer)
							end
						end

						fprintf('\nFinal streak: %i\n', streak)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-while-rps">
			<title>Rock, Paper, Scissors</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Build a Rock, Paper, Scissors game that you play against the computer. The game uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to keep playing rounds until one player builds a winning lead.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-rps-a">
				<title>Single round</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that plays one round of Rock, Paper, Scissors. The player enters their choice as a number (1 = Rock, 2 = Paper, 3 = Scissors) using <pf>input</pf>. The computer picks randomly with <pf>randi([1, 3])</pf>. Use <pf>if-elseif-else</pf> or <pf>switch</pf> to determine the winner. Print both choices and the result.
					</p>
					<p>
						<em>Hint:</em> There are three cases where the player wins: (Rock vs Scissors), (Paper vs Rock), and (Scissors vs Paper). You can handle ties separately and treat everything else as a computer win.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-rps-b">
				<title>First to 3 wins</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Wrap your single round in a <pf>while</pf>-loop so the game continues until either the player or the computer reaches 3 wins. Track scores in variables <pf>playerScore</pf> and <pf>compScore</pf>. Ties should not count as wins for either side. After the match, announce the winner.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						playerScore = 0;
						compScore = 0;
						names = {'Rock', 'Paper', 'Scissors'};

						fprintf('=== Rock, Paper, Scissors — First to 3! ===\n\n')

						while playerScore &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; compScore &lt; 3
							fprintf('Score: You %i - %i Computer\n', playerScore, compScore)
							player = input('Enter 1=Rock, 2=Paper, 3=Scissors: ');
							comp = randi([1, 3]);

							fprintf('  You: %s, Computer: %s\n', names{player}, names{comp})

							if player == comp
								fprintf('  Tie!\n\n')
							elseif (player == 1 &amp;&amp; comp == 3) || ...
										(player == 2 &amp;&amp; comp == 1) || ...
										(player == 3 &amp;&amp; comp == 2)
								fprintf('  You win this round!\n\n')
								playerScore = playerScore + 1;
							else
								fprintf('  Computer wins this round!\n\n')
								compScore = compScore + 1;
							end
						end

						fprintf('=== Final Score: You %i - %i Computer ===\n', playerScore, compScore)
						if playerScore == 3
							fprintf('You win the match!\n')
						else
							fprintf('Computer wins the match.\n')
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-rps-c">
				<title>Stats tracking</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add variables to track the total number of rounds played and the number of ties. After the match ends, display a summary that includes total rounds, wins, losses, and ties.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution. Add these lines before the loop:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						rounds = 0;
						ties = 0;
					</program>
					<p>
						Increment <pf>rounds</pf> each iteration and <pf>ties</pf> in the tie branch. After the loop:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						fprintf('\n=== Match Summary ===\n')
						fprintf('Total rounds: %i\n', rounds)
						fprintf('Your wins:    %i\n', playerScore)
						fprintf('Comp wins:    %i\n', compScore)
						fprintf('Ties:         %i\n', ties)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-while-change-machine">
			<title>Change-Making Machine</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					When a vending machine gives you change, it uses a <term>greedy algorithm</term>: it always picks the largest coin that fits, then repeats. This is one of the most classic examples of a <pf>while</pf>-loop in everyday programming.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-change-machine-a">
				<title>Quarters only</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a <pf>while</pf>-loop that determines how many quarters (25¢) fit into a given amount in cents, and what the remaining balance is. For example, 87 cents contains 3 quarters with 12 cents remaining. Do <em>not</em> use division or <pf>floor</pf> — subtract 25 repeatedly in the loop.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							amount = 87;
							quarters = 0;
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							

							% =============================
							fprintf('%i quarters, %i cents left\n', quarters, amount)
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						amount = 87;
						quarters = 0;
						% ==== Type your code here ====
						while amount &gt;= 25
							amount = amount - 25;
							quarters = quarters + 1;
						end
						% =============================
						fprintf('%i quarters, %i cents left\n', quarters, amount)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-change-machine-b">
				<title>Full change breakdown</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Extend your script to break a given amount into quarters (25¢), dimes (10¢), nickels (5¢), and pennies (1¢), using a separate <pf>while</pf>-loop for each coin type. For example, 87 cents should produce 3 quarters, 1 dime, 0 nickels, and 2 pennies.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							amount = 87;
							quarters = 0; dimes = 0; nickels = 0; pennies = 0;
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							

							% =============================
							fprintf('Quarters: %i\n', quarters)
							fprintf('Dimes:    %i\n', dimes)
							fprintf('Nickels:  %i\n', nickels)
							fprintf('Pennies:  %i\n', pennies)
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						amount = 87;
						quarters = 0; dimes = 0; nickels = 0; pennies = 0;
						% ==== Type your code here ====
						while amount &gt;= 25
							amount = amount - 25;
							quarters = quarters + 1;
						end
						while amount &gt;= 10
							amount = amount - 10;
							dimes = dimes + 1;
						end
						while amount &gt;= 5
							amount = amount - 5;
							nickels = nickels + 1;
						end
						pennies = amount;
						% =============================
						fprintf('Quarters: %i\n', quarters)
						fprintf('Dimes:    %i\n', dimes)
						fprintf('Nickels:  %i\n', nickels)
						fprintf('Pennies:  %i\n', pennies)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-change-machine-c">
				<title>Minimizing coins</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add a variable <pf>totalCoins</pf> that tracks the total number of coins used. The greedy algorithm always produces the fewest coins for US currency. Verify this by testing with amounts like 99, 41, and 30.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="activity-while-password">
			<title>PIN Lockout System</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Many real systems lock a user out after too many failed login attempts. In this activity, you will build a simple PIN verification system with limited attempts. <em>Note:</em> This activity uses the <pf>input</pf> command and must be run in MATLAB (not in the browser).
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="activity-password-a">
				<title>Unlimited attempts</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a script that stores a secret PIN (e.g., <pf>pin = 1234</pf>) and uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to repeatedly prompt the user for a guess using <pf>input('Enter PIN: ')</pf>. If the guess is wrong, print an error message and ask again. If correct, print a welcome message and exit the loop.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						pin = 1234;
						guess = -1;

						while guess ~= pin
							guess = input('Enter PIN: ');
							if guess ~= pin
								fprintf('Incorrect. Try again.\n')
							end
						end

						fprintf('Access granted. Welcome!\n')
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-password-b">
				<title>Three strikes</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Modify your script so the user gets at most 3 attempts. Use a compound condition in the <pf>while</pf>-loop. After the loop, use an <pf>if-else</pf> to display either a welcome message or a lockout warning depending on whether the PIN was entered correctly.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Here is one possible solution:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						pin = 1234;
						guess = -1;
						attempts = 0;
						maxAttempts = 3;

						while guess ~= pin &amp;&amp; attempts &lt; maxAttempts
							fprintf('Attempts remaining: %i\n', maxAttempts - attempts)
							guess = input('Enter PIN: ');
							attempts = attempts + 1;
							if guess ~= pin
								fprintf('Incorrect.\n')
							end
						end

						if guess == pin
							fprintf('Access granted. Welcome!\n')
						else
							fprintf('Account locked. Contact support.\n')
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="activity-password-c">
				<title>Cooldown warning</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add a feature: after each failed attempt, display how many attempts remain. On the second failure, print an additional warning: <pf>"Warning: 1 attempt remaining before lockout."</pf>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>

		</exercise>
		
	</reading-questions>

</section>